      <section class="hero">
        <div class="wrap hero-grid">
          <div>
            <div class="kicker">Work &amp; Productivity · Meetings</div>
            <h1>Turn meeting notes into clear action items.</h1>
            <p class="lede">Use this prompt to turn rough or unstructured meeting notes into decisions, assigned actions, deadlines and questions that still need resolving.</p>
            <div class="hero-meta"><span>Best for meeting follow-up</span><span>Reusable template</span><span>Works with pasted notes</span></div>
          </div>

          <aside class="summary-card">
            <img class="mark" src="/brand/promptendium-mark.webp" alt="" width="303" height="303" />
            <h2>What you get</h2>
            <p>A structured follow-up containing decisions, action items, owners, dates, unresolved questions and a short summary.</p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="page">
        <div class="wrap layout">
          <article class="article">
            <div class="prompt-shell" id="prompt">
              <div class="prompt-head">
                <div><strong>Copy-ready prompt</strong><br /><span>Replace the bracketed fields before sending</span></div>
                <button class="copy" type="button" id="copyButton">Copy prompt</button>
              </div>
              <div class="prompt-body">
                <pre class="prompt-text" id="promptText">{promptText}</pre>
                <div class="var-note"><strong>Why the placeholders matter:</strong> the prompt gives the model enough context to organise the notes without encouraging it to fill missing information with guesses.</div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <section class="section" id="what">
              <div class="kicker">Purpose</div>
              <h2>What this prompt does</h2>
              <p>Meeting notes often mix decisions, observations, half-finished ideas and tasks. This prompt separates those types of information and asks the model to preserve uncertainty rather than silently turning incomplete notes into confident statements.</p>
              <p>The result is intended as a first structured draft for human review, not as proof that every extracted action was agreed.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="section" id="use">
              <div class="kicker">Instructions</div>
              <h2>How to use it</h2>
              <div class="steps">
                <div class="step"><div class="step-num">1</div><div><strong>Add the meeting context</strong><span>Enter the project or team name and, where useful, the purpose of the meeting.</span></div></div>
                <div class="step"><div class="step-num">2</div><div><strong>Paste the raw notes</strong><span>They can be rough. Keep names, dates and decisions exactly as recorded where possible.</span></div></div>
                <div class="step"><div class="step-num">3</div><div><strong>Review missing owners and dates</strong><span>The prompt deliberately marks unsupported information as “Not specified” instead of guessing.</span></div></div>
                <div class="step"><div class="step-num">4</div><div><strong>Check before sending</strong><span>Confirm the final actions and commitments against the actual meeting before circulating the follow-up.</span></div></div>
              </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section" id="customise">
              <div class="kicker">Customise</div>
              <h2>Useful ways to adapt the prompt</h2>
              <div class="table">
                <div class="row"><div class="label">For a client meeting</div><div class="value">Add: “Separate actions owned by our team from actions owned by the client.”</div></div>
                <div class="row"><div class="label">For a project review</div><div class="value">Add: “Include a separate Risks / blockers section and identify any action that affects the critical path.”</div></div>
                <div class="row"><div class="label">For a short follow-up email</div><div class="value">Replace the final summary instruction with: “Finish with a concise email-ready summary under 150 words.”</div></div>
                <div class="row"><div class="label">For recurring meetings</div><div class="value">Add the previous action list and ask the model to distinguish carried-over actions from newly agreed actions.</div></div>
              </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section" id="example">
              <div class="kicker">Example</div>
              <h2>What a good result should look like</h2>
              <div class="example">
                <div class="example-box">
                  <div class="small">Example notes</div>
                  <pre>Launch page ready Friday.
Sam to confirm analytics before launch.
Pricing copy still needs approval.
Maybe move customer email to Monday?
Jen checking final wording.</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="example-box output">
                  <div class="small">Structured output</div>
                  <pre>Decisions
• Launch page targeted for Friday.

Action items
• Confirm analytics — Sam — deadline not specified.
• Check final pricing wording — Jen — deadline not specified.

Open questions
• Is the customer email moving to Monday?
• Who gives final pricing-copy approval?</pre>
                </div>
              </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section" id="why">
              <div class="kicker">Prompt design</div>
              <h2>Why this prompt is structured this way</h2>
              <p>The instructions distinguish confirmed information from uncertainty, specify an output schema, and explicitly forbid unsupported owners or deadlines. Those constraints make the output easier to review and reduce the chance that an incomplete note becomes an invented commitment.</p>
              <p>The table format also gives a consistent structure that can be pasted into a project update, email or task-management workflow.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="section" id="related">
              <div class="kicker">Continue</div>
